just tell us a little bit about what  what happened down in mar-a-lago last  night um president trump has called it a  raid was it such  i don't think that's the right word jane  it's a court authorized search warrant  that means the department of justice the  fbi  went to a  federal judge a neutral and detached  magistrate and swore out a warrant which  demonstrated probable cause for two  things one that a crime had been  committed and second that they would  find evidence of that crime at the place  they wanted to search in this case  mr trump's florida home so i think a  raid is a  an inaccurate and unfortunate term it's  a court authorized search warrant they  were lawfully present because a judge  said they could be there  so what were they looking for  right so we don't entirely know because  the search warrant is under seal it will  be unsealed at some point but right now  it remains sealed and that's standard  protocol  they're looking for evidence of a crime  now which crime and what evidence that  will all be specified in the warrant and  when we see it we'll know more about  that piece of it  so that actual looking for evidence of a  crime is quite interesting isn't it so  it's it's not simply the case as far as  you're aware that they're just looking  to recover documents they're they are  actually looking for evidence of a crime  or is it the case that the fact that the  documents that should be in the national  archives are there is a crime in itself  well the documents in and of themselves  could be the evidence of the crime but  to get a search warrant under u.s law  and by the way our fourth amendment uh  comes from english common law i mean the  antecedents were the objections of the  colonists to english authorities using  what were called general writs of  assistance to do searches at any home at  any time and so our fourth amendment  narrowed that right it's much like  english common law now you have to have  a specific crime and you have to tell  the judge who is in a different branch  of our government so two branches of our  government are involved in a search  warrant you have to tell the judge what  it is you're looking for that would be a  violation of u.s federal law so it is  rather specific we just don't have the  specifics yet jane  but but we we are assuming that that  it's documentation that relates to his  time as president and that that  documents  those should have been lodged in the  national archives why why is it  important why is there this precedent  that these kind of notes or bits of  paper that the president uses go to this  special place  well two things first we have something  called the presidential records act  which requires presidents to turn over  their  official papers to our national archives  but second and i think more important  here and this goes to your very good  question  if the documents are classified at a  high level  and disclosure of them could do grave  damage to u.s national security  interests well then it's a crime  actually there are a suite of crimes  related to mishandling or improperly  retaining or in the worst case  transmitting classified information so  that's the crime at least the potential  crime you can't mishandle retain  transmit  classified information as a former  president mr trump no longer has the  authority to classify or declassify  information nor can he retain it and so  um that could be the underlying reason  why the fbi went to a judge and asked  for the search warrant  and has this ever happened before he  says that it's been unprecedented that a  president has been treated like this  well  to my knowledge unprecedented that uh  the home of a former president would be  searched  but very much precedent for the notion  that if you break the law regardless of  who you are  then you suffer the consequences right  now the consequence is a search warrant  again  authorized by a federal judge and  lawfully executed whether or not he's  charged i have absolutely no idea jane  but there was a determination made by a  federal judge that there was probable  cause to believe a crime would become  had been committed and that you would  find evidence at his home so in that  sense  this is what happens when people commit  crimes what is unprecedented is it  happens to be a potential crime by a  former president of the united states  and just a final thought chuck when the  fbi go in on these operations to recover  stuff is it is anything off limits we  we've had president trump's son saying  that that that this safe was i think it  is broken into but i mean  when they get a warrant for to search a  property can they pretty much go  anywhere  yeah so that's a great question jane let  me explain it this way if you had a  search warrant for my home because  you're looking for a stolen car you  couldn't look in my refrigerator and you  couldn't look in my safe it wouldn't  make sense that you'd find a stolen car  in one of those places but if you have a  search warrant for documents a safe is a  very logical place and so when the fbi  has a search warrant that includes  things that could be in a safe then they  lawfully have access to the safe ideally  the homeowner gives the combination to  the safe and the fbi doesn't have to  break into it but if they did break into  it it's because they had lawful access  to it and so i think mr trump's son is  making um a bit of a fuss here uh and i  understand why they might be upset but  this is pursuant to law it was properly  authorized and it seems to me properly  executed  chuck rosenberg former u.s attorney and  former senior fbi official thank you  very much  thank you
 

